5, -0.01351165771484375, 0.034881591796875, -0.0271759033203125, 0.0001195669174194336, -0.0390625, -0.03070068359375, -0.01953125, -0.031463623046875, 0.0322265625, 0.0309906005859375, 0.0215301513671875, 0.0164947509765625, 0.02081298828125, -0.01107025146484375, 0.01220703125, -0.04754638671875, 0.00846099853515625, -0.0003464221954345703, 0.01430511474609375, 0.034637451171875, -0.039764404296875, -0.004131317138671875, -0.01438140869140625, -0.016326904296875, 0.0034847259521484375, 0.01276397705078125, 0.00258636474609375, 0.0198822021484375, 0.0026569366455078125, 0.006877899169921875, 0.0032444000244140625, 0.030059814453125, -0.01412200927734375, 0.01435089111328125, -0.01473236083984375, 0.008514404296875, 0.0182037353515625, -0.02447509765625, -0.005496978759765625, -0.0029010772705078125, -0.0289154052734375, -0.01238250732421875, -0.006679534912109375, 0.0153961181640625, -0.0279693603515625, 0.015411376953125, -0.0036640167236328125, 0.0179901123046875, -0.00905609130859375, -0.00739288330078125, -0.01293182373046875, 0.01593017578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. Our contribution is to provide new evidence on these questions using the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), extending its application to a novel game environment in addition to the established beauty contest setting. In this method, we define \"level-0 actions\" as the choices made by participants who, based on within-team discussions, demonstrate no strategic reasoning about others' actions, and \"level-0 beliefs\" as their stated expectations about the choices of other similarly non-strategic players.\n\nWe focus on two classic games: the beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game (Goeree et al. 2018). The beauty contest game requires players to choose a number between 0 and 100, with the winner being the player whose choice is closest to a fixed fraction (e.g., two-thirds) of the average of all choices; it is widely used for studying iterative and boundedly rational reasoning. The variant of the 11-20 game offers each player an integer choice between 11 and 20, with payoffs structured so that higher numbers yield sure but smaller payoffs while lower numbers carry the risk of coordination failure, making first-mover thinking ill-advised under standard level-k assumptions. These games are chosen because they offer contrasting contexts in which to observe whether \"level-0\" behavior, as operationalized in the intra-team communication method, aligns with predictions from standard models.\n\nIn the beauty contest, we replicate Burchardi and Penczynski's (2014) result that elicited level-0 beliefs\u2014participants\u2019 naive expectations about others\u2019 play\u2014correspond closely to level-0 actions: teams classified as level-0 both expect and choose numbers near the center of the available interval (around 50), consistent with the conventional assumption that level-0 behavior is uniform or \u201cnaive.\u201d In contrast, in the 11-20 game variant, both elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions diverge from this canonical level-0 prediction: many level-0 teams choose and expect higher numbers (close to 20), but not uniformly so, and some show risk-avoidant or focal responses. These findings indicate that participants\u2019 level-0 behavior is sensitive to the structure and payoffs of the game.\n\nOur results suggest a complementary explanation to that of Goeree et al. (2018): such behavioral patterns arise not from failures of iterative reasoning per se, but from flexible, game-dependent anchors in level-0 reasoning. This complements the standard level-k model by highlighting that the \u201crandomness\u201d or \u201cnaivete\u201d ascribed to level-0 behavior may itself be systematically shaped by the environment and payoff structure. Collectively, the convergence of beliefs and actions in the beauty contest game and their divergence in the variant 11-20 game indicate that the relation between level-0 beliefs and level-0 actions is game-dependent, pointing to the need for more nuanced modeling of level-0 types. Our findings challenge the standard assumption of a game-independent, uniformly random or unstrategic level-0 type, and suggest that elicited beliefs and actions from intra-team communication can offer valuable insights for refining the level-k framework."}}